window.META_MATH = {"subject": "Real Analysis", "projects": [{"id": "RA-01", "slug": "intermediate-value-theorem", "title": "Intermediate Value Theorem", "target": "<p>For continuous\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>,\nprove that every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y</annotation></semantics></math>\nbetween\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)</annotation></semantics></math>\nequals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(c)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor some\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\in[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use the least-upper-bound property of R and epsilon–delta continuity.\nA continuous function at c preserves a strict sign on a sufficiently\nsmall relative neighborhood. No intermediate-value or root-existence\ntheorem is imported.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Sign reduction and endpoints", "proof": "1. If y=f(a) or f(b), the corresponding endpoint solves the problem.\n\n2. Otherwise put g=f−y and, if necessary, replace g by−g so g(a)<0<g(b).\n\n3. Define S={x∈[a,b]:g(x)<0}.\n\n4. It contains a and is bounded above by b, hence c=supS exists.", "role": "<p>Translate and possibly reverse the sign.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Continuous f on[a,b]; y between endpoint values; foundational imports\nstated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>A supremum candidate c for the strict-sign case.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: A supremum candidate c for the strict-sign case.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Continuous f on[a,b]; y between endpoint values; foundational\nimports stated in §1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Translate and possibly reverse the sign.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Endpoint equalities must be closed before assuming strict signs.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe corresponding endpoint solves the problem.</p>", "<p>Otherwise put\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=f-y</annotation></semantics></math>\nand, if necessary, replace g by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-g</annotation></semantics></math>\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(a)&lt;0&lt;g(b)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>:</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=\\{x\\in [a,b]:g(x)&lt;0\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>It contains a and is bounded above by b, hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sup</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\operatorname{sup} S</annotation></semantics></math>\nexists.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Endpoint equalities must be closed before assuming strict signs.\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Endpoint equalities must be closed\nbefore assuming strict signs. Recheck dependent nodes N2, N3.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Exclude a negative value at c", "proof": "1. Continuity at b and g(b)>0 give a relative neighborhood of b with positive g, so c<b.\n\n2. If g(c)<0, continuity gives δ>0 with g negative near c.\n\n3. Choose x with c<x<min(b,c+δ), so x∈S contradicting the upper-bound property.\n\n4. Thus g(c)≥0.", "role": "<p>A negative value would allow a larger point in S.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Supremum candidate; continuity; prior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Supremum candidate; continuity; prior exports E1/v1; keep\ntheir assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>A negative value would allow a larger point in S.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Use the endpoint sign to ensure there is room to the right.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Continuity at b and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(b)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\ngive a relative neighborhood of b with positive g, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncontinuity gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta &gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith g negative near c.</p>", "<p>Choose x with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&lt;x&lt;\\operatorname{min} (b,c+\\delta )</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in S</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontradicting the upper-bound property.</p>", "<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Use the endpoint sign to ensure there is room to the right. Publish\nonly after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Use the endpoint sign to ensure there\nis room to the right. Recheck dependent nodes N3.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Exclude a positive value at c", "proof": "1. Continuity at a gives some points strictly greater than a in S, so c>a.\n\n2. If g(c)>0, g is positive on(c−δ,c+δ)∩[a,b].\n\n3. By the defining property of supremum there is s∈S with c−δ<s≤c, a contradiction.\n\n4. Hence g(c)≤0.\n\n5. Together the inequalities force g(c)=0; undoing the sign reduction gives f(c)=y.", "role": "<p>Approximate the supremum from below.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Supremum and nonempty strict-negative neighborhood of a; prior\nexports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p>Every intermediate endpoint value is attained.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E3/v1: Every intermediate endpoint value is attained.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Supremum and nonempty strict-negative neighborhood of a; prior\nexports E1/v1, E2/v1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Approximate the supremum from below.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Being a supremum alone does not imply belonging to S.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Continuity at a gives some points strictly greater than a in S, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,\ng is positive on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(c-\\delta ,c+\\delta )\\cap [a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>By the defining property of supremum there is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\in S</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c-\\delta &lt;s\\le c</annotation></semantics></math>,\na contradiction.</p>", "<p>Hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)\\le 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Together the inequalities force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)=0</annotation></semantics></math>;\nundoing the sign reduction gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(c)=y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>Being a supremum alone does not imply belonging to S. Publish only\nafter checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: Being a supremum alone does not imply\nbelonging to S. Withhold this terminal result until its gate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Exclude a negative value at c", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Supremum candidate; continuity; prior\nexports E1/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N1", "target": "N3", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Exclude a positive value at c", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Supremum and nonempty strict-negative\nneighborhood of a; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T3", "activity": "N3: Exclude a positive value at c", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Supremum and nonempty strict-negative\nneighborhood of a; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-sign-reduction-and-endpoints-1\">§4.N1 — Sign reduction and\nendpoints</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe corresponding endpoint solves the problem.</p></li>\n<li><p>Otherwise put\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=f-y</annotation></semantics></math>\nand, if necessary, replace g by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-g</annotation></semantics></math>\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(a)&lt;0&lt;g(b)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>:</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=\\{x\\in [a,b]:g(x)&lt;0\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>It contains a and is bounded above by b, hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sup</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\operatorname{sup} S</annotation></semantics></math>\nexists.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-exclude-a-negative-value-at-c-1\">§4.N2 — Exclude a negative\nvalue at c</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Continuity at b and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(b)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\ngive a relative neighborhood of b with positive g, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncontinuity gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta &gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith g negative near c.</p></li>\n<li><p>Choose x with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&lt;x&lt;\\operatorname{min} (b,c+\\delta )</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in S</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontradicting the upper-bound property.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-exclude-a-positive-value-at-c-1\">§4.N3 — Exclude a positive\nvalue at c</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Continuity at a gives some points strictly greater than a in S,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,\ng is positive on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(c-\\delta ,c+\\delta )\\cap [a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>By the defining property of supremum there is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\in S</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c-\\delta &lt;s\\le c</annotation></semantics></math>,\na contradiction.</p></li>\n<li><p>Hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)\\le 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Together the inequalities force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(c)=0</annotation></semantics></math>;\nundoing the sign reduction gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(c)=y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^{3}-x-1</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1)=-1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(2)=5</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe supremum construction yields a root in (1,2). Bisection checks\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1.25</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>0.296875</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1.25)=-0.296875</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.875</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1.5)=0.875</annotation></semantics></math>,\nnarrowing the interval; these computations illustrate the theorem but\nare not its universal proof.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>The jump\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=-1</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand 1 for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">on[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas both signs and misses0. At its supremum candidate continuity fails.\nSense rejects the sign-preservation step; no bracket computation repairs\nthe original discontinuous theorem.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Ego\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Intellect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Sense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory + Intellect:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Ego:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>The jump\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(x)=-1</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand 1 for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">on[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas both signs and misses0. At its supremum candidate continuity fails.\nSense rejects the sign-preservation step; no bracket computation repairs\nthe original discontinuous theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "f350b198b70d87d85d1082a57f40bc98e5a8f05da1528b33529d95cc7086c373", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of intermediate value theorem, with\nexplicit mathematical contracts, local verification gates, and reusable\nexports.</p>", "references": "", "pdf": "reports/pdf/intermediate-value-theorem.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/intermediate-value-theorem.tex", "markdown": "markdown/intermediate-value-theorem.md"}, {"id": "RA-02", "slug": "heineborel-theorem", "title": "Heine–Borel Theorem", "target": "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\subset\\mathbb R^n</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith its Euclidean topology, prove that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math>\nis compact if and only if it is closed and bounded.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use completeness of R to prove compactness of closed boxes below, the\nEuclidean distance, and finite-volume geometry. Compact means every open\ncover has a finite subcover. The proof supplies the needed closed-box\nlemma rather than importing Heine–Borel itself.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Closed boxes are compact", "proof": "1. Suppose a box Q has an open cover with no finite subcover.\n\n2. Bisect each side; at least one of the2ⁿ closed subboxes has no finite subcover, since otherwise their finite covers unite.\n\n3. Continue to nested boxes Q_j whose diameters tend to 0.\n\n4. Completeness in each coordinate supplies a common point x; it is unique by the diameter bound.\n\n5. A covering open set contains a ball B(x,ε).\n\n6. For large j every point of Q_j is within its diameter of x, so Q_j lies in that one set, contradiction.\n\n7. Degenerate sides cause no difficulty.", "role": "<p>Use nested bisection and completeness.</p>", "inputs": "<p>An open cover of a closed box in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nfoundational imports stated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>Every closed bounded box is compact.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: Every closed bounded box is compact.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain An open cover of a closed box in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>;\nfoundational imports stated in §1; keep their assumptions and versions\nattached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Use nested bisection and completeness.</p>", "Sense": "<p>The cover sets are open in an ambient neighborhood; nested endpoints\nmust be retained.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Suppose a box Q has an open cover with no finite subcover.</p>", "<p>Bisect each side; at least one of the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nclosed subboxes has no finite subcover, since otherwise their finite\ncovers unite.</p>", "<p>Continue to nested boxes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nwhose diameters tend to 0.</p>", "<p>Completeness in each coordinate supplies a common point x; it is\nunique by the diameter bound.</p>", "<p>A covering open set contains a ball\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(x,\\varepsilon )</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>For large j every point of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nis within its diameter of x, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in that one set, contradiction.</p>", "<p>Degenerate sides cause no difficulty.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>The cover sets are open in an ambient neighborhood; nested endpoints\nmust be retained. Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: The cover sets are open in an ambient\nneighborhood; nested endpoints must be retained. Recheck dependent nodes\nN3.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Compact sets are bounded and closed", "proof": "1. The increasing balls B(0,j), j≥1, cover K; a finite subcover places it in one sufficiently large ball.\n\n2. For x∉K and each y∈K choose disjoint balls U_y around y and V_y around x, using distance(x,y)>0.\n\n3. Finitely many U_y cover K.\n\n4. The intersection of the corresponding V_y is an open neighborhood of x disjoint from K.\n\n5. Thus the complement of K is open and K is closed.\n\n6. The empty set satisfies both conclusions directly.", "role": "<p>Use a ball cover and point separation.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Compact K and Euclidean Hausdorff geometry; foundational imports\nstated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>Compact K is closed and bounded.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1: Compact K is closed and bounded.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Compact K and Euclidean Hausdorff geometry; foundational\nimports stated in §1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Use a ball cover and point separation.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Boundedness alone does not supply a finite subcover.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>The increasing balls\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(0,j)</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j\\ge 1</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncover K; a finite subcover places it in one sufficiently large ball.</p>", "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\notin K</annotation></semantics></math>\nand each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\in K</annotation></semantics></math>\nchoose disjoint balls\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_y</annotation></semantics></math>\naround y and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_y</annotation></semantics></math>\naround x, using distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Finitely many\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_y</annotation></semantics></math>\ncover K.</p>", "<p>The intersection of the corresponding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_y</annotation></semantics></math>\nis an open neighborhood of x disjoint from K.</p>", "<p>Thus the complement of K is open and K is closed.</p>", "<p>The empty set satisfies both conclusions directly.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Boundedness alone does not supply a finite subcover. Publish only\nafter checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Boundedness alone does not supply a\nfinite subcover. Recheck dependent nodes N3.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Closed bounded sets are compact", "proof": "1. Place K in a closed box Q.\n\n2. Given a relative open cover of K, represent its members as K∩O_i with ambient O_i open.\n\n3. The O_i together with Rⁿ\\K cover Q.\n\n4. Compactness of Q yields finitely many sets; discarding the complement leaves finitely many O_i covering K.\n\n5. This proves compactness and joins both directions of the equivalence.", "role": "<p>Adjoin the complement to an open cover.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Closed bounded K; compact box; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\subset R^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis compact iff closed and bounded.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E3/v1:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\subset R^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis compact iff closed and bounded.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Closed bounded K; compact box; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1;\nkeep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Adjoin the complement to an open cover.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Relative open sets must be converted correctly to a cover of Q.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Place K in a closed box Q.</p>", "<p>Given a relative open cover of K, represent its members as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\cap O_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith ambient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nopen.</p>", "<p>The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O_i</annotation></semantics></math>\ntogether with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>\\</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^{n}\\setminus K</annotation></semantics></math>\ncover Q.</p>", "<p>Compactness of Q yields finitely many sets; discarding the complement\nleaves finitely many\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O_i</annotation></semantics></math>\ncovering K.</p>", "<p>This proves compactness and joins both directions of the\nequivalence.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>Relative open sets must be converted correctly to a cover of Q.\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: Relative open sets must be converted\ncorrectly to a cover of Q. Withhold this terminal result until its gate\npasses.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N3", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N3: Closed bounded sets are compact", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Closed bounded K; compact box; prior exports\nE1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Closed bounded sets are compact", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Closed bounded K; compact box; prior exports\nE1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-closed-boxes-are-compact-1\">§4.N1 — Closed boxes are\ncompact</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Suppose a box Q has an open cover with no finite\nsubcover.</p></li>\n<li><p>Bisect each side; at least one of the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nclosed subboxes has no finite subcover, since otherwise their finite\ncovers unite.</p></li>\n<li><p>Continue to nested boxes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nwhose diameters tend to 0.</p></li>\n<li><p>Completeness in each coordinate supplies a common point x; it is\nunique by the diameter bound.</p></li>\n<li><p>A covering open set contains a ball\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(x,\\varepsilon )</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>For large j every point of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nis within its diameter of x, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in that one set, contradiction.</p></li>\n<li><p>Degenerate sides cause no difficulty.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-compact-sets-are-bounded-and-closed-1\">§4.N2 — Compact sets\nare bounded and closed</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>The increasing balls\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(0,j)</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j\\ge 1</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncover K; a finite subcover places it in one sufficiently large\nball.</p></li>\n<li><p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\notin K</annotation></semantics></math>\nand each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\in K</annotation></semantics></math>\nchoose disjoint balls\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_y</annotation></semantics></math>\naround y and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_y</annotation></semantics></math>\naround x, using distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,y)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Finitely many\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_y</annotation></semantics></math>\ncover K.</p></li>\n<li><p>The intersection of the corresponding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_y</annotation></semantics></math>\nis an open neighborhood of x disjoint from K.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus the complement of K is open and K is closed.</p></li>\n<li><p>The empty set satisfies both conclusions directly.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-closed-bounded-sets-are-compact-1\">§4.N3 — Closed bounded\nsets are compact</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Place K in a closed box Q.</p></li>\n<li><p>Given a relative open cover of K, represent its members as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K\\cap O_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith ambient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O_i</annotation></semantics></math>\nopen.</p></li>\n<li><p>The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O_i</annotation></semantics></math>\ntogether with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>\\</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^{n}\\setminus K</annotation></semantics></math>\ncover Q.</p></li>\n<li><p>Compactness of Q yields finitely many sets; discarding the\ncomplement leaves finitely many\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O_i</annotation></semantics></math>\ncovering K.</p></li>\n<li><p>This proves compactness and joins both directions of the\nequivalence.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=[0,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe nested-box proof applies to every open cover, not just a grid. In\ncontrast (0,1) is covered by set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(1/j,1),j\\ge 2</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith no finite subcover; it is bounded but not closed in R.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>The unit coordinate vectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nin the closed unit ball of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell ^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\nhave mutual distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso there is no convergent subsequence. The infinite-dimensional analogue\nof the box argument has no\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">finite2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nsubdivision and fails. Sense preserves the finite-dimensional Euclidean\nscope.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Ego\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Intellect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Sense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory + Intellect:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Ego:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>The unit coordinate vectors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nin the closed unit ball of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell ^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>\nhave mutual distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso there is no convergent subsequence. The infinite-dimensional analogue\nof the box argument has no\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">finite2^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nsubdivision and fails. Sense preserves the finite-dimensional Euclidean\nscope.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "a7c07f2c840952e46af90e3a413afe47678838fb1f31ccf9d2f72714de37b8d3", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of heine–borel theorem, with explicit\nmathematical contracts, local verification gates, and reusable\nexports.</p>", "references": "", "pdf": "reports/pdf/heineborel-theorem.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/heineborel-theorem.tex", "markdown": "markdown/heineborel-theorem.md"}, {"id": "RA-03", "slug": "banach-fixed-point-theorem", "title": "Banach Fixed-Point Theorem", "target": "<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(X,d)</annotation></semantics></math>\nbe a nonempty complete metric space and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T:X\\to X</annotation></semantics></math>\nsatisfy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(Tx,Ty)\\le qd(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x,y</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwith a fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le q&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThen there is a unique\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_*</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Tx_*=x_*</annotation></semantics></math>.\nFrom any\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=Tx_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nconverges to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_*</annotation></semantics></math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;q&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_n,x_*)\\le\\frac{q^n}{1-q}d(x_1,x_0).</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The output is an existence proof, a uniqueness proof, a convergent\nconstruction, and an error certificate. An iterate with small\nconsecutive displacement is an approximation; it is not automatically an\nexact fixed point.</p>", "imports": ["<p>completeness means every Cauchy sequence in X has a limit in X; a\ncontraction has one uniform factor q; geometric-series summation and the\ntriangle inequality are imported. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nT is constant, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1</annotation></semantics></math>\nis already a fixed point. We prove the remaining case\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;q&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "An Admissible Infinite Construction", "proof": "Choose $x_0\\in X$ and put $x_{n+1}=Tx_n$. Every iterate stays in X because T is a self-map. Set $D=d(x_1,x_0)$. If D=0, the initial point is already fixed. Otherwise the following estimates still apply without changing the algorithm.", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: For every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=T(x_n)</annotation></semantics></math>\nstays in the nonempty complete space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Metric and map definitions</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Choose any starting point</p>", "Sense": "<p>Check completeness, self-map,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>An Admissible Infinite Construction. Establish the following local\nresult: For every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=T(x_n)</annotation></semantics></math>\nstays in the nonempty complete space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T:X\\to X</annotation></semantics></math>\nand a uniform contraction constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le q&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "export": "<p>For every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=T(x_n)</annotation></semantics></math>\nstays in the nonempty complete space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Check nonemptiness, completeness, the self-map condition, and a\nsingle global constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe map is constant and its value is its unique fixed point.</p>", "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;q&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,\nfix any\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand form the iteration.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>Check completeness, self-map,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: Check completeness, self-map,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>\nRecheck dependent nodes N2, N3, N4, N5.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Shrinking Increments and the Cauchy Certificate", "proof": "Induction using the contraction gives\n\n$$d(x_{n+1},x_n)\\le q^nD.$$\n\nFor $m>n$, the triangle inequality therefore gives\n\n$$d(x_m,x_n)\\le\\sum_{k=n}^{m-1}q^kD\\le\\frac{q^nD}{1-q}.$$\n\nThe bound tends to zero independently of m. Given any epsilon, choose n large enough that the last expression is smaller than epsilon. Thus the sequence is Cauchy. This is a statement about every sufficiently late pair of iterates, not just adjacent ones.", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_{n+p},x_n)\\le q^n d(x_1,x_0)/(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math>;\nthe iterates are Cauchy.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Geometric series</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Sum shrinking increments</p>", "Sense": "<p>Keep q uniform</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Shrinking Increments and the Cauchy Certificate. Establish the\nfollowing local result:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_{n+p},x_n)\\le q^n d(x_1,x_0)/(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math>;\nthe iterates are Cauchy.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N1/E1 and the finite geometric-series bound.</p>", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_{n+p},x_n)\\le q^n d(x_1,x_0)/(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math>;\nthe iterates are Cauchy.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Inductively prove\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_{n+1},x_n)\\le q^n d(x_1,x_0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Sum increments from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+p</annotation></semantics></math>\nusing the triangle inequality.</p>", "<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>\ngrow while keeping the tail estimate uniform in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Keep q uniform Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Keep q uniform Recheck dependent nodes\nN3, N5.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Completeness and the Fixed-Point Bridge", "proof": "Completeness supplies $x_*\\in X$ with $x_n\\to x_*$. Since\n\n$$d(Tx_*,x_*)\\le d(Tx_*,Tx_n)+d(x_{n+1},x_*)\\le qd(x_*,x_n)+d(x_{n+1},x_*),$$\n\nthe right side tends to zero. Hence $Tx_*=x_*$. Letting m tend to infinity in N2's estimate gives the stated error bound. The continuity used here follows from the contraction inequality itself.", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E3/v1: There exists\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_*\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(x_*)=x_*</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_n,x_*)\\le q^n d(x_1,x_0)/(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Completeness</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Use contraction continuity</p>", "Sense": "<p>Keep limit in X</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Completeness and the Fixed-Point Bridge. Establish the following\nlocal result: There exists\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_*\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(x_*)=x_*</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_n,x_*)\\le q^n d(x_1,x_0)/(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N2/E2 and completeness of the original metric space.</p>", "export": "<p>There exists\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_*\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(x_*)=x_*</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_n,x_*)\\le q^n d(x_1,x_0)/(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Take the Cauchy limit inside\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(Tx_*,x_*)\\le qd(x_*,x_n)+d(x_{n+1},x_*)</annotation></semantics></math>\nand pass to the limit.</p>", "<p>Let the tail endpoint tend to infinity in the estimate.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>Keep limit in X Publish only after checking every displayed\ndeduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: Keep limit in X Recheck dependent\nnodes N5.</p>"}, {"id": "N4", "title": "Uniqueness", "proof": "If x and y are fixed points, then $d(x,y)=d(Tx,Ty)\\le qd(x,y)$. Consequently $(1-q)d(x,y)\\le0$. As $1-q>0$ and distance is nonnegative, $d(x,y)=0$, so x=y.", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E4/v1: Any two fixed points in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>\nare equal.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Distance axioms</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Apply contraction to their distance</p>", "Sense": "<p>Compare exact fixed points</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E4/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Uniqueness. Establish the following local result: Any two fixed\npoints in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>\nare equal.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N1/E1; two putative exact fixed points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u,v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "export": "<p>Any two fixed points in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math>\nare equal.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Apply the contraction inequality to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u,v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-q)d(u,v)\\le0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand the metric separation axiom.</p>"], "exportId": "E4", "gate": "<p>Compare exact fixed points Publish only after checking every\ndisplayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N4: Compare exact fixed points Recheck\ndependent nodes N5.</p>"}, {"id": "N5", "title": "Synthesis and a Stopping Certificate", "proof": "Every initial point has a limit by N2–N3, and N4 shows all those limits are the same point. For a practical stopping criterion, N2 applied starting at $x_n$ gives\n\n$$d(x_n,x_*)\\le\\frac{d(x_{n+1},x_n)}{1-q}.$$\n\nFor n≥1 there is also the sharper bound\n\n$$d(x_n,x_*)\\le\\frac{q}{1-q}d(x_n,x_{n-1}).$$\n\nThus a requested tolerance can be met using observed increments and a proved contraction factor. This completes the theorem. $\\square$", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E5/v1: The unique fixed point is reached in the limit from every\nstarting point, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_n,x_*)\\le d(x_n,x_{n+1})/(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>All predecessor certificates</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Join construction and bounds</p>", "Sense": "<p>Separate approximation from equality</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E5/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "role": "<p>Synthesis and a Stopping Certificate. Establish the following local\nresult: The unique fixed point is reached in the limit from every\nstarting point, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_n,x_*)\\le d(x_n,x_{n+1})/(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>N2/E2, N3/E3, and N4/E4.</p>", "export": "<p>The unique fixed point is reached in the limit from every starting\npoint, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_n,x_*)\\le d(x_n,x_{n+1})/(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Restart the geometric-tail estimate at index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Use the observed next increment to produce a certified stopping\ntolerance.</p>", "<p>Combine existence, uniqueness, and both error estimates without\nequating a finite iterate to the exact limit.</p>"], "exportId": "E5", "gate": "<p>Separate approximation from equality Publish only after checking\nevery displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N5: Separate approximation from equality\nWithhold this terminal result until its gate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Shrinking Increments and the Cauchy Certificate", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1 and the finite geometric-series\nbound.</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Completeness and the Fixed-Point Bridge", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N2/E2 and completeness of the original\nmetric space.</p>"}, {"source": "N1", "target": "N4", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T3", "activity": "N4: Uniqueness", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N1/E1; two putative exact fixed points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u,v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N5", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T4", "activity": "N5: Synthesis and a Stopping Certificate", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N2/E2, N3/E3, and N4/E4.</p>"}, {"source": "N3", "target": "N5", "export": "E3/v1", "id": "T5", "activity": "N5: Synthesis and a Stopping Certificate", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N2/E2, N3/E3, and N4/E4.</p>"}, {"source": "N4", "target": "N5", "export": "E4/v1", "id": "T6", "activity": "N5: Synthesis and a Stopping Certificate", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: N2/E2, N3/E3, and N4/E4.</p>"}], "proof": "<h3 id=\"n1-an-admissible-infinite-construction-1\">N1 — An Admissible\nInfinite Construction</h3>\n<p>Choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>\nand put\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=Tx_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nEvery iterate stays in X because T is a self-map. Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=d(x_1,x_0)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe initial point is already fixed. Otherwise the following estimates\nstill apply without changing the algorithm.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n2-shrinking-increments-and-the-cauchy-certificate-1\">N2 —\nShrinking Increments and the Cauchy Certificate</h3>\n<p>Induction using the contraction gives</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>D</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_{n+1},x_n)\\le q^nD.</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m&gt;n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe triangle inequality therefore gives</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>D</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>D</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_m,x_n)\\le\\sum_{k=n}^{m-1}q^kD\\le\\frac{q^nD}{1-q}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The bound tends to zero independently of m. Given any epsilon, choose\nn large enough that the last expression is smaller than epsilon. Thus\nthe sequence is Cauchy. This is a statement about every sufficiently\nlate pair of iterates, not just adjacent ones.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n3-completeness-and-the-fixed-point-bridge-1\">N3 — Completeness\nand the Fixed-Point Bridge</h3>\n<p>Completeness supplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_*\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_n\\to x_*</annotation></semantics></math>.\nSince</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(Tx_*,x_*)\\le d(Tx_*,Tx_n)+d(x_{n+1},x_*)\\le qd(x_*,x_n)+d(x_{n+1},x_*),</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>the right side tends to zero. Hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Tx_*=x_*</annotation></semantics></math>.\nLetting m tend to infinity in N2’s estimate gives the stated error\nbound. The continuity used here follows from the contraction inequality\nitself.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n4-uniqueness-1\">N4 — Uniqueness</h3>\n<p>If x and y are fixed points, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x,y)=d(Tx,Ty)\\le qd(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nConsequently\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-q)d(x,y)\\le0</annotation></semantics></math>.\nAs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand distance is nonnegative,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x,y)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n5-synthesis-and-a-stopping-certificate-1\">N5 — Synthesis and a\nStopping Certificate</h3>\n<p>Every initial point has a limit by N2–N3, and N4 shows all those\nlimits are the same point. For a practical stopping criterion, N2\napplied starting at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_n</annotation></semantics></math>\ngives</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_n,x_*)\\le\\frac{d(x_{n+1},x_n)}{1-q}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge 1</annotation></semantics></math>\nthere is also the sharper bound</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>*</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x_n,x_*)\\le\\frac{q}{1-q}d(x_n,x_{n-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Thus a requested tolerance can be met using observed increments and a\nproved contraction factor. This completes the theorem.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>▫</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\square</annotation></semantics></math></p>", "example": "<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith ordinary distance and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(x)=\\operatorname{cos}</annotation></semantics></math>\nx. The interval is complete, and T maps it into [cos 1,1], a subset of\nX. The mean value theorem gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\cos x-\\cos y|\\le(\\sin1)|x-y|</annotation></semantics></math>.\nUse\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.841471</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\sin1\\approx0.841471&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p>Starting from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>n</th>\n<th>Approximate iterate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0.540302306</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>0.857553216</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>0.654289790</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0.793480359</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The alternation does not invalidate convergence. A contraction bound\ncontrols its shrinking oscillations. After 100 iterations, the a priori\nerror bound is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>100</mn></msup><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2.1</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q^{100}/(1-q)&lt;2.1\\times10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe limit is approximately 0.7390851332; this decimal is illustrative,\nwhile the theorem establishes the unique exact solution in [0,1].</p>", "boundary": ["<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Boundary</th>\n<th>Consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>T is constant; one application reaches the fixed point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Identity on a multi-point space has many fixed points; uniqueness\nfails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Missing completeness</td>\n<td>Limit may leave the space</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Map leaves X</td>\n<td>Iteration is not covered by the contract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Finite decimal equality</td>\n<td>Does not certify an exact fixed point</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Export the contraction theorem, uniqueness lemma, and both error\nestimates with their hypotheses. The <a\nhref=\"../../advanced-calculus/projects/index.html#AC-04\">Inverse\nFunction Theorem example</a> imports this completed module to solve\nnearby equations.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>Consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(x)=x/2</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nA proposed branch claims a fixed point because the distances halve.\nSense finds that X is not complete: the iterates converge to 0, which is\nmissing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> N3</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>State:</strong> STALLED</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Failing faculty:</strong> Sense</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Failed hypothesis:</strong> complete domain containing\nthe limit</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Example:</strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(x)=x/2</annotation></semantics></math>\non (0,1)</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Repair target:</strong> N1</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Blocked export:</strong> fixed-point existence in\n(0,1)</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Memory retrieves completeness’s definition. Sense retains the\nmissing-point diagnosis. Intellect proposes [0,1] for this example.\nAction verifies that the repaired domain is complete and invariant and\nthat 0 is fixed. Ego publishes a theorem for the repaired domain while\nrecording that the original map on (0,1) has no fixed point. Repair does\nnot retroactively prove the rejected original claim.</p>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Boundary</th>\n<th>Consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>T is constant; one application reaches the fixed point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Identity on a multi-point space has many fixed points; uniqueness\nfails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Missing completeness</td>\n<td>Limit may leave the space</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Map leaves X</td>\n<td>Iteration is not covered by the contract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Finite decimal equality</td>\n<td>Does not certify an exact fixed point</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Export the contraction theorem, uniqueness lemma, and both error\nestimates with their hypotheses. The <a\nhref=\"../../advanced-calculus/projects/index.html#AC-04\">Inverse\nFunction Theorem example</a> imports this completed module to solve\nnearby equations.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": false, "sha256": "806b19d3b8d7aade97d162d6c1c5ca88619bf75c52995f9a01ff432ff0e55293", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of banach fixed-point theorem, with\nexplicit mathematical contracts, local verification gates, and reusable\nexports.</p>", "references": "<p><a\nhref=\"https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~gressman/analysis/12-inversefuncthm.html\">University\nof Pennsylvania advanced-analysis material on contraction-based\ninversion</a>.</p>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/banach-fixed-point-theorem.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/banach-fixed-point-theorem.tex", "markdown": "markdown/banach-fixed-point-theorem.md"}, {"id": "RA-04", "slug": "lebesgue-dominated-convergence-theorem", "title": "Lebesgue Dominated Convergence Theorem", "target": "<p>On a measure space, let measurable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n\\to f</annotation></semantics></math>\nalmost everywhere and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f_n|\\le g</annotation></semantics></math>\nalmost everywhere for all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\in L^1</annotation></semantics></math>\nis nonnegative. Prove\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\in L^1</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|f_n-f\\|_1\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int f_n\\to\\int f</annotation></semantics></math>.\nWe use a measurable representative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>\nof the almost-everywhere limit.</p>", "imports": ["<p>On a measure space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(X,\\Sigma ,\\mu )</annotation></semantics></math>,\nFatou’s lemma says\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">liminf</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int \\operatorname{liminf}</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">liminf</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n\\le \\operatorname{liminf}</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int h_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor nonnegative measurable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nUse countable unions of null sets and the integral triangle inequality.\nFor complex-valued functions, apply measurability to real and imaginary\nparts; the same absolute-value proof works.</p>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>\nto be a measurable representative of the almost-everywhere limit; all\nexceptional sets are contained in a measurable null set. This convention\navoids silently assuming the measure space is complete.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Common full-measure domain", "proof": "1. Take the union of the convergence exceptional set and the countably many bound exceptions.\n\n2. It is null.\n\n3. On its complement every bound holds and f_n→f; redefine f and f_n on that null set if needed.\n\n4. A pointwise limit of measurable functions is measurable, and |f|≤g by continuity of absolute value.\n\n5. Thus ∫|f|≤∫g<∞; every f_n is integrable too.", "role": "<p>Remove all exceptional null sets at once.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Measurable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n</annotation></semantics></math>;\na.e. convergence; a.e. bounds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f_n|\\le g</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>\nintegrable; foundational imports stated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>The limit and all terms are integrable; all bounds hold on one\nfull-measure set.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: The limit and all terms are integrable; all bounds hold on\none full-measure set.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Measurable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n</annotation></semantics></math>;\na.e. convergence; a.e. bounds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f_n|\\le g</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>\nintegrable; foundational imports stated in §1; keep their assumptions\nand versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Remove all exceptional null sets at once.</p>", "Sense": "<p>A separate exceptional set for each n must be consolidated.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Take the union of the convergence exceptional set and the countably\nmany bound exceptions.</p>", "<p>It is null.</p>", "<p>On its complement every bound holds and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n\\to f</annotation></semantics></math>;\nredefine f and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n</annotation></semantics></math>\non that null set if needed.</p>", "<p>A pointwise limit of measurable functions is measurable, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f|\\le g</annotation></semantics></math>\nby continuity of absolute value.</p>", "<p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int |f|\\le \\int g&lt;\\infty</annotation></semantics></math>;\nevery\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nis integrable too.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>A separate exceptional set for each n must be consolidated. Publish\nonly after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: A separate exceptional set for each n\nmust be consolidated. Recheck dependent nodes N2, N3.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "L¹ convergence from Fatou", "proof": "1. The triangle inequality gives 0≤|f_n−f|≤2g, so h_n≥0 and h_n→2g almost everywhere.\n\n2. Fatou yields 2∫g≤liminf(2∫g−∫|f_n−f|)=2∫g−limsup∫|f_n−f|.\n\n3. All quantities subtracted are finite because g∈L¹.\n\n4. Hence the nonnegative error integrals have limsup0 and converge to 0.", "role": "<p>Apply Fatou to a nonnegative error complement.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Integrable f and bounds;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n=2g-|f_n-f|</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert f_n-f\\Vert _{1}\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert f_n-f\\Vert _{1}\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Integrable f and bounds;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n=2g-|f_n-f|</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior exports E1/v1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Apply Fatou to a nonnegative error complement.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Avoid\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∞</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty -\\infty</annotation></semantics></math>;\nfiniteness of the dominating integral is used explicitly.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>The triangle inequality gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le |f_n-f|\\le 2g</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n\\to 2g</annotation></semantics></math>\nalmost everywhere.</p>", "<p>Fatou yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">liminf</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">limsup</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\int g\\le \\operatorname{liminf} (2\\int g-\\int |f_n-f|)=2\\int g-\\operatorname{limsup}</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int |f_n-f|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>All quantities subtracted are finite because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\in L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Hence the nonnegative error integrals have limsup0 and converge to\n0.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>Avoid\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∞</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty -\\infty</annotation></semantics></math>;\nfiniteness of the dominating integral is used explicitly. Publish only\nafter checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: Avoid\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∞</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty -\\infty</annotation></semantics></math>;\nfiniteness of the dominating integral is used explicitly. Recheck\ndependent nodes N3.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Integral convergence", "proof": "1. Linearity and the triangle inequality imply |∫f_n−∫f|=|∫(f_n−f)|≤∫|f_n−f|→0.\n\n2. This proves the full contract, including integrability of the limit.\n\n3. The proof does not claim pointwise convergence at exceptional points or uniform convergence.", "role": "<p>Control the integral difference by its absolute error.</p>", "inputs": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nerror bound; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int f_n\\to \\int f</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\in L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nconvergence.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E3/v1:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int f_n\\to \\int f</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\in L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nconvergence.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nerror bound; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1; keep their assumptions and\nversions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Control the integral difference by its absolute error.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Pointwise convergence alone is not the gate for an integral\nexchange.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Linearity and the triangle inequality imply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\int f_n-\\int f|=|\\int (f_n-f)|\\le \\int |f_n-f|\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>This proves the full contract, including integrability of the\nlimit.</p>", "<p>The proof does not claim pointwise convergence at exceptional points\nor uniform convergence.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>Pointwise convergence alone is not the gate for an integral exchange.\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: Pointwise convergence alone is not the\ngate for an integral exchange. Withhold this terminal result until its\ngate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: L¹ convergence from Fatou", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Integrable f and bounds;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n=2g-|f_n-f|</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior exports E1/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N1", "target": "N3", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Integral convergence", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nerror bound; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T3", "activity": "N3: Integral convergence", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nerror bound; prior exports E1/v1, E2/v1</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-common-full-measure-domain-1\">§4.N1 — Common full-measure\ndomain</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Take the union of the convergence exceptional set and the\ncountably many bound exceptions.</p></li>\n<li><p>It is null.</p></li>\n<li><p>On its complement every bound holds and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n\\to f</annotation></semantics></math>;\nredefine f and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n</annotation></semantics></math>\non that null set if needed.</p></li>\n<li><p>A pointwise limit of measurable functions is measurable, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f|\\le g</annotation></semantics></math>\nby continuity of absolute value.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int |f|\\le \\int g&lt;\\infty</annotation></semantics></math>;\nevery\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nis integrable too.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n=2g-|f_n-f|\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math>\nafter modifying on the common measurable null set.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n2-l1-convergence-from-fatou-1\">§4.N2 —\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nconvergence from Fatou</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>The triangle inequality gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le |f_n-f|\\le 2g</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n\\ge 0</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_n\\to 2g</annotation></semantics></math>\nalmost everywhere.</p></li>\n<li><p>Fatou yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">liminf</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">limsup</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\int g\\le \\operatorname{liminf} (2\\int g-\\int |f_n-f|)=2\\int g-\\operatorname{limsup}</annotation></semantics></math>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int |f_n-f|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>All quantities subtracted are finite because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\in L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Hence the nonnegative error integrals have limsup0 and converge\nto 0.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-integral-convergence-1\">§4.N3 — Integral convergence</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Linearity and the triangle inequality imply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo>∫</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\int f_n-\\int f|=|\\int (f_n-f)|\\le \\int |f_n-f|\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>This proves the full contract, including integrability of the\nlimit.</p></li>\n<li><p>The proof does not claim pointwise convergence at exceptional\npoints or uniform convergence.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>On[0,1],\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n(x)=x^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis dominated by 1 and converges to 0 except at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0</annotation></semantics></math>\nas an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nclass;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int x^{n}=1/(n+1)\\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>,\nmatching the theorem. Uniform convergence fails because the supremum\nremains1.</p>", "boundary": ["<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">On(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n=n\\cdot 1_{0,1/n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nconverges pointwise to 0 while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int f_n=1</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIf an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\ndominator existed DCT would contradict this calculation; directly its\nrequired lower bounds force nonintegrable growth near0. Sense blocks the\ncommon-dominator claim rather than modifying the limit calculation.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Ego\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Intellect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Sense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory + Intellect:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Ego:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">On(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n=n\\cdot 1_{0,1/n}</annotation></semantics></math>\nconverges pointwise to 0 while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int f_n=1</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIf an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>\ndominator existed DCT would contradict this calculation; directly its\nrequired lower bounds force nonintegrable growth near0. Sense blocks the\ncommon-dominator claim rather than modifying the limit calculation.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "ea70dc39c188088694ceb7d43e6c28ab2e74037dbc83914bde1bb42fa9714359", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of lebesgue dominated convergence\ntheorem, with explicit mathematical contracts, local verification gates,\nand reusable exports.</p>", "references": "<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-125-measure-and-integration-fall-2003/pages/lecture-notes/\">Fatou\nand dominated convergence: MIT Measure and Integration</a>.</li>\n</ul>", "pdf": "reports/pdf/lebesgue-dominated-convergence-theorem.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/lebesgue-dominated-convergence-theorem.tex", "markdown": "markdown/lebesgue-dominated-convergence-theorem.md"}, {"id": "RA-05", "slug": "arzelaascoli-theorem", "title": "Arzelà–Ascoli Theorem", "target": "<p>For compact metric\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math>,\nprove that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℱ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal F\\subset C(K,\\mathbb R)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis relatively compact in the supremum norm if and only if it is\nuniformly bounded and equicontinuous.</p>", "imports": ["<p>For compact metric K, finite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-nets\nexist. Bolzano–Weierstrass applies to bounded real sequences.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(K)</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith supremum norm is complete; a uniform limit of continuous functions\nis continuous. In metric spaces compactness equals sequential\ncompactness, and complete totally bounded spaces are compact. These\nelementary metric lemmas are permitted imports.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Diagonal pointwise subsequence", "proof": "1. Assume K nonempty; if it is empty the function space has one element.\n\n2. Unite finite1/j-nets to obtain a countable dense set{z₁,z₂,…}.\n\n3. Given any sequence in F, repeatedly extract subsequences converging atz_j using boundedness and Bolzano–Weierstrass.\n\n4. The diagonal subsequence g_j converges at every fixed z_i.", "role": "<p>Exploit a countable dense set supplied by finite nets.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Uniform boundedness and equicontinuity of F; foundational imports\nstated in §1</p>", "export": "<p>A subsequence converges at all points of a countable dense set.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E1/v1: A subsequence converges at all points of a countable dense\nset.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Uniform boundedness and equicontinuity of F; foundational\nimports stated in §1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Exploit a countable dense set supplied by finite nets.</p>", "Sense": "<p>A dense set alone does not imply uniform convergence.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E1/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Assume K nonempty; if it is empty the function space has one\nelement.</p>", "<p>Unite finite1/j-nets to obtain a countable dense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">set\\{z_{1},z_{2},\\ldots \\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Given any sequence in F, repeatedly extract subsequences converging\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nusing boundedness and Bolzano–Weierstrass.</p>", "<p>The diagonal subsequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nconverges at every fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "exportId": "E1", "gate": "<p>A dense set alone does not imply uniform convergence. Publish only\nafter checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N1: A dense set alone does not imply\nuniform convergence. Recheck dependent nodes N2, N3, N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Uniform Cauchy bridge", "proof": "1. Given ε>0 choose δ so d(x,y)<δ implies |h(x)−h(y)|<ε/3 for every h∈F.\n\n2. Choose a finiteδ-net whose centers lie in the dense set.\n\n3. For large j,k all finitely many center differences |g_j(z_i)−g_k(z_i)| are belowε/3.\n\n4. For arbitrary x choose a center withinδ; the triangle inequality gives |g_j(x)−g_k(x)|<ε.\n\n5. Completeness of C(K) supplies a uniform continuous limit.\n\n6. Every sequence in F has such a subsequence.", "role": "<p>Control the entire space by finitely many nearby sampled points.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Diagonal convergence; uniform equicontinuity; prior exports E1/v1</p>", "export": "<p>Every sequence in F has a uniformly convergent subsequence in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(K)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E2/v1: Every sequence in F has a uniformly convergent subsequence\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(K)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Diagonal convergence; uniform equicontinuity; prior exports\nE1/v1; keep their assumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Control the entire space by finitely many nearby sampled points.</p>", "Sense": "<p>The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nis common to the family, not chosen separately for each function.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E2/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon &gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nchoose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x,y)&lt;\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nimplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|h(x)-h(y)|&lt;\\varepsilon /3</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\in F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Choose a finite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-net\nwhose centers lie in the dense set.</p>", "<p>For large j,k all finitely many center differences\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|g_j(z_i)-g_k(z_i)|</annotation></semantics></math>\nare below\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon /3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>For arbitrary x choose a center within\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>;\nthe triangle inequality gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|g_j(x)-g_k(x)|&lt;\\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Completeness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(K)</annotation></semantics></math>\nsupplies a uniform continuous limit.</p>", "<p>Every sequence in F has such a subsequence.</p>"], "exportId": "E2", "gate": "<p>The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nis common to the family, not chosen separately for each function.\nPublish only after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N2: The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nis common to the family, not chosen separately for each function.\nRecheck dependent nodes N3, N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Relative compactness", "proof": "1. For a sequence h_j in the closure of F choose g_j∈F with ‖h_j−g_j‖∞<1/j.\n\n2. A uniformly convergent subsequence of g_j forces the corresponding h_j to converge to the same limit, which lies in the closed set closure(F).\n\n3. Its closure is therefore sequentially compact and hence compact by the metric lemma.\n\n4. This proves sufficiency.", "role": "<p>Extend the property to the closure.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Subsequence property; prior exports E2/v1</p>", "export": "<p>Uniform boundedness and equicontinuity imply relative\ncompactness.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E3/v1: Uniform boundedness and equicontinuity imply relative\ncompactness.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Subsequence property; prior exports E2/v1; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Extend the property to the closure.</p>", "Sense": "<p>A limit need not belong to F itself, only its closure.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E3/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>For a sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nin the closure of F choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_j\\in F</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert h_j-g_j\\Vert \\infty &lt;1/j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>A uniformly convergent subsequence of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nforces the corresponding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nto converge to the same limit, which lies in the closed set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (F)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Its closure is therefore sequentially compact and hence compact by\nthe metric lemma.</p>", "<p>This proves sufficiency.</p>"], "exportId": "E3", "gate": "<p>A limit need not belong to F itself, only its closure. Publish only\nafter checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N3: A limit need not belong to F itself,\nonly its closure. Recheck dependent nodes N4.</p>"}, {"id": "N4", "title": "Necessity from finite norm nets", "proof": "1. Cover closure(F) by finitely many norm balls of radius1 centered at h_i.\n\n2. Then ‖f‖∞≤1+max_i‖h_i‖∞.\n\n3. For ε>0 choose a finite norm net of radiusε/3 with continuous centers h_i.\n\n4. Each center is uniformly continuous on K; choose one δ valid for all finitely many.\n\n5. For d(x,y)<δ and f withinε/3 of h_i, |f(x)−f(y)|≤2‖f−h_i‖∞+|h_i(x)−h_i(y)|<ε.\n\n6. Thus F is equicontinuous; both directions close.", "role": "<p>Turn compactness into uniform bounds and a shared continuity\nmodulus.</p>", "inputs": "<p>Compact\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (F)</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior export E3/v1 for the final equivalence synthesis</p>", "export": "<p>Relative compactness is equivalent to both stated family\nconditions.</p>", "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Own E4/v1: Relative compactness is equivalent to both stated family\nconditions.</p>", "Memory": "<p>Retain Compact\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (F)</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior export E3/v1 for the final equivalence synthesis; keep their\nassumptions and versions attached.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Turn compactness into uniform bounds and a shared continuity\nmodulus.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Pointwise boundedness is not the hypothesis used in the diagonal step\nunless compact equicontinuity is separately used to upgrade it.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out the numbered deductions and publish E4/v1 together with its\nhypotheses and proof location.</p>"}, "activities": ["<p>Cover\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (F)</annotation></semantics></math>\nby finitely many norm balls of radius 1 centered at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert f\\Vert \\infty \\le 1+\\operatorname{max} _i\\Vert h_i\\Vert \\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon &gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nchoose a finite norm net of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon /3</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith continuous centers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Each center is uniformly continuous on K; choose one\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nvalid for all finitely many.</p>", "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x,y)&lt;\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nand f within\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon /3</annotation></semantics></math>\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_i</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f(x)-f(y)|\\le 2\\Vert f-h_i\\Vert \\infty +|h_i(x)-h_i(y)|&lt;\\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Thus F is equicontinuous; both directions close.</p>"], "exportId": "E4", "gate": "<p>Pointwise boundedness is not the hypothesis used in the diagonal step\nunless compact equicontinuity is separately used to upgrade it. Publish\nonly after checking every displayed deduction.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the check fails, revise N4: Pointwise boundedness is not the\nhypothesis used in the diagonal step unless compact equicontinuity is\nseparately used to upgrade it. Withhold this terminal result until its\ngate passes.</p>"}], "edges": [{"source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "id": "T1", "activity": "N2: Uniform Cauchy bridge", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Diagonal convergence; uniform\nequicontinuity; prior exports E1/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "id": "T2", "activity": "N3: Relative compactness", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Subsequence property; prior exports\nE2/v1</p>"}, {"source": "N3", "target": "N4", "export": "E3/v1", "id": "T3", "activity": "N4: Necessity from finite norm nets", "gate": "<p>Use the same mathematical objects and hypotheses as the source\nexport. Receiving contract: Compact\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (F)</annotation></semantics></math>;\nprior export E3/v1 for the final equivalence synthesis</p>"}], "proof": "<p>Read these steps in order as one proof; the diagram is optional.\nNumbered activities identify the specific constructions and deductions\nowned by each node.</p>\n<h3 id=\"n1-diagonal-pointwise-subsequence-1\">§4.N1 — Diagonal pointwise\nsubsequence</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Assume K nonempty; if it is empty the function space has one\nelement.</p></li>\n<li><p>Unite finite1/j-nets to obtain a countable dense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">set\\{z_{1},z_{2},\\ldots \\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Given any sequence in F, repeatedly extract subsequences\nconverging at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nusing boundedness and Bolzano–Weierstrass.</p></li>\n<li><p>The diagonal subsequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nconverges at every fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-uniform-cauchy-bridge-1\">§4.N2 — Uniform Cauchy bridge</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon &gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nchoose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x,y)&lt;\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nimplies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|h(x)-h(y)|&lt;\\varepsilon /3</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\in F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Choose a finite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-net\nwhose centers lie in the dense set.</p></li>\n<li><p>For large j,k all finitely many center differences\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|g_j(z_i)-g_k(z_i)|</annotation></semantics></math>\nare below\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon /3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>For arbitrary x choose a center within\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>;\nthe triangle inequality gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|g_j(x)-g_k(x)|&lt;\\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Completeness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(K)</annotation></semantics></math>\nsupplies a uniform continuous limit.</p></li>\n<li><p>Every sequence in F has such a subsequence.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-relative-compactness-1\">§4.N3 — Relative compactness</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>For a sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nin the closure of F choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_j\\in F</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert h_j-g_j\\Vert \\infty &lt;1/j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>A uniformly convergent subsequence of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nforces the corresponding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_j</annotation></semantics></math>\nto converge to the same limit, which lies in the closed set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (F)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Its closure is therefore sequentially compact and hence compact\nby the metric lemma.</p></li>\n<li><p>This proves sufficiency.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n4-necessity-from-finite-norm-nets-1\">§4.N4 — Necessity from\nfinite norm nets</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Cover\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">closure</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{closure} (F)</annotation></semantics></math>\nby finitely many norm balls of radius 1 centered at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max</mi><mo>&#8289;</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Vert f\\Vert \\infty \\le 1+\\operatorname{max} _i\\Vert h_i\\Vert \\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon &gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>\nchoose a finite norm net of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon /3</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith continuous centers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Each center is uniformly continuous on K; choose one\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nvalid for all finitely many.</p></li>\n<li><p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d(x,y)&lt;\\delta</annotation></semantics></math>\nand f within\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon /3</annotation></semantics></math>\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_i</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖</mo><mi>∞</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f(x)-f(y)|\\le 2\\Vert f-h_i\\Vert \\infty +|h_i(x)-h_i(y)|&lt;\\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thus F is equicontinuous; both directions close.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>The functions on[0,1] with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f|\\le 1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand Lipschitz constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\le 1</annotation></semantics></math>\nare equicontinuous, so every sequence has a uniform convergent\nsubsequence. The proof takes common\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta =\\varepsilon /3</annotation></semantics></math>\nand finitely many rational grid centers; the limit keeps the same\nbounds.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>The family\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\mapsto x^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\non[0,1] is uniformly bounded but not equicontinuous at 1. Its only\npossible pointwise limit has a jump at 1, so no subsequence converges\nuniformly. Sense rejects treating boundedness as sufficient and routes\nthe failure to the common-modulus node.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Failure and correction", "html": "<p>This is an authored teaching trace, not a recorded agent run.</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Ego\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nMemory:</strong> register the §1 theorem, imports, and requested exports\nbefore beginning the proof.</li>\n<li><strong>Intellect\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nAction:</strong> choose the bridges in §3 and carry out the numbered\nactivities in §4; transmit only established E-exports along the §2\nDAG.</li>\n<li><strong>Sense\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to</annotation></semantics></math>\nresponsible node:</strong> test the specific boundary/failure in §5 and\nthe node’s individual audit. A failing candidate is withheld; downstream\nnodes may not use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory + Intellect:</strong> identify whether the error is\nan omitted condition, an invalid inference, or an attempted extension\nbeyond the theorem. Restore the exact contract or explicitly state a\ndifferent one.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> redo the affected mathematical activity\nusing the repaired condition or inference. Recheck consuming nodes,\nrather than feeding their conclusions back as premises.</li>\n<li><strong>Ego:</strong> publish only the supported export with its\nassumptions and proof location. A hypothetical revised export is v2; the\nwritten proof here is the v1 exposition, not a claim that this repair\nwas executed.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"label": "Hypothesis boundary", "html": "<p>The family\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\mapsto x^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>\non[0,1] is uniformly bounded but not equicontinuous at 1. Its only\npossible pointwise limit has a jump at 1, so no subsequence converges\nuniformly. Sense rejects treating boundedness as sufficient and routes\nthe failure to the common-modulus node.</p>"}, {"label": "Version and recheck", "html": "<p>Treat any changed assumption as a new contract. Recheck the revised\nexport and all of its descendants. A backward diagnostic never\nestablishes an earlier theorem. This is an authored teaching trace, not\nan execution log.</p>"}], "standard": true, "sha256": "c123886f518b1203624b8c162e42a1cd6d1a078b073e1cfa0161c1d57225c64c", "idea": "<p>A complete authored walkthrough of arzelà–ascoli theorem, with\nexplicit mathematical contracts, local verification gates, and reusable\nexports.</p>", "references": "", "pdf": "reports/pdf/arzelaascoli-theorem.pdf", "tex": "reports/latex/arzelaascoli-theorem.tex", "markdown": "markdown/arzelaascoli-theorem.md"}], "links": [], "evidence": "Authored proof walkthroughs. No deployed agent execution or formal kernel verification is claimed."};
